1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hanuman: An Epic Hero
2 CONT
2 CONT Hanuman: The Devotee of Lord Rama IT is hard to find a character who is at once so powerful, learned, philosophic, humble and amusing! Hanuman features prominently in the great epics of Ramayana and the Mahabharata. Hanuman Meets 
2 CONC Rama Hanuman met Rama and his brother Lakshmana while Rama was in exile in the jungle, and searching for his wife Sita who was abducted by Ravana. Their quest brought them near Pampa Lake at the foot of Mount Risyamukha, where the
2 CONC  monkey king Sugriva and his ministers were hiding. Sugriva, who was being persecuted by his brother Bali, suspected that Rama and Lakshmana might have been sent by Bali to slay him. To find out the facts, Hanuman approached them 
2 CONC in the guise of a Brahmin. In Service of Rama Hanuman's initial words highly impressed Rama, and made him comment: "None can talk this way without mastering the Vedas. He has such a flawless countenance, a wonderful accent, and a 
2 CONC captivating way of speaking. He has the ability to move even an enemy..." After he revealed his identity as the prince of Ayodhya, Hanuman fell prostrate before him in respect of the Lord. Rama picked him up and embraced him. Ther
2 CONC e began the story of Hanuman, which is inextricably interwoven with Rama, and dealt with in detail in Valmiki'sRamayana and the Tulsidas' Ramacharitamanas. To cut the long story of Hanuman short, he then introduced Rama to Sugriva
2 CONC , and began his massive search for Sita. Finding out her whereabouts, he consoled Sita, and burnt down the city of Lanka. Hanuman then brought Rama to Lanka, fought the battle against Ravana with his simian army, and vanquished th
2 CONC e demons. Hanuman's greatest feat was saving the life of Lakshmana by fetching the life-giving herb "Sanjivani" from the Himalayas. He flew fast towards the Himalayas, but unable to recognise the right herb, picked up the whole mo
2 CONC untain on his hand and flew back to Lanka, just in time to save Lakshmana. Thereafter Hanuman served Rama forever. Hanuman and the Pandavas The venerable ape also features in the great epic Mahabharata. How Hanuman met the valiant
2 CONC  Bhima, one of the Pandava brothers is itself a marvelous tale. He recognized Bhima as his spiritual brother, since both were born with the blessings of Pavana, the Wind God, and promised to aid the Pandavas in the big battle of K
2 CONC urukshetra. Hanuman positioned himself in the flag of Arjuna's chariot to secure and stabilize the war-craft. The triangular saffron flag of Hanuman stands for stability and equilibrium, sense-control and mind-control, and a sure 
2 CONC sign of victory over all that is base and evil.
2 CONT
2 CONT . Hanuman (IPA: hʌnʊˈmɑn) is a Hindu deity, who was an ardent devotee of Rama according to the Hindu legends. He is a central character in the Indian epicRamayana, and also finds mentions in several other texts, including Mahabhar
2 CONC ata, the various Puranas and some Jain texts. A vanara (ape-like humanoid), Hanuman participated in Rama's war against the demon king Ravana. Several texts also present him as an incarnation of the Lord Shiva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etymology and other names Hanuman
2 CONT
2 CONT Indonesian Balinese wooden statue of Hanuman The Sanskrit texts mention several legends about how Hanuman got his name. One legend is that Indra, the king of the deities, struck Hanuman's jaw during his childhood (see below). The 
2 CONC child received his name from the Sanskrit words Hanu ("jaw") and -man (or -mant, "prominent" or "disfigured"). The name thus means "one with prominent or disfigured jaw".[1] Another theory says the name derives from the Sanskrit w
2 CONC ords Han ("killed" or "destroyed") andmaana (pride); the name implies "one whose pride was destroyed".[1] Some Jain texts mention that Hanuman spent his childhood on an island called Hanuruha, which is the origin of his name.[2] A
2 CONC ccording to one theory, the name "Hanuman" derives from the proto-Dravidian word for male monkey (ana-mandi), which was later Sanskritized to "Hanuman" (see historical development below). Linguistic variations of  Hanuman
2 CONT
2 CONT Raja Ravi Varma's lithograph of Hanuman fetching the mountain
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanaras
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanskrit Transliteration हनुमान् Affiliation	Devotee of Rama
2 CONT
