2 CONT Fearing problems with Gandhari's pregnancy which had continued for over a year, Dhritrashtra impregnated a palace maid servant who attended to him. Ironically, he was born younger to Duryodhan but elder than Gandhari's second son 
2 CONC Dushasan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus Dhritrashtra, contrary to popular perception had 101 sons, not 100.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was among the 11 athirathi (capable of fighting 10,000 warriors at a time) among the Kauravs. He was the only one from Duryodhan's family and well wishers who was against Duryodhan's actions. He did not fight from Duryodhan's s
2 CONC ide but from Paandav's side. Before war, he tried his best to explain Duryodhan not to quarrel with Pandavas but Duryodhan wouldn't listen to anybody. When Pandavas left the world, Yudhishthir installed him as the king of Indrapra
2 CONC sth
2 CONT
2 CONT The evil-minded and wicked king Duryodhana, the destroyer of the fair fame of the Kurus, was born of a portion of Kali on earth. He it was who caused all creatures to be slain and the earth to be wasted; and he it was who fanned t
2 CONC he flame of hostility that ultimately consumed all. They who had been the sons of Pulastya (the Rakshasas) were born on earth among men of Duryodhana's brothers, that century of wicked individuals commencing with Duhasasana as the
2 CONC ir first. And, O bull among the Bharata princes, Durmukha, Duhsaha, and others whose names I do not mention, who always supported Duryodhana (in all his schemes), were, indeed, the sons of Pulastya. And over and above these hundre
2 CONC d, Dhritarashtra had one son named Yuyutsu born of a Vaisya wife.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "Janamejaya said, 'O illustrious one, tell me the names of Dhritarashtra's sons according to the order of their birth beginning from the eldest.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "Vaisampayana said, 'O king, they are as follows: Duryodhana, and Yuyutsu, and also Duhsasana; Duhsaha and Duhshala, and then Durmukha;
2 CONT
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Vivinsati, and Vikarna, Jalasandha, Sulochna, Vinda and Anuvinda, Durdharsha, Suvahu, Dushpradharshana; Durmarshana, and Dushkarna, and Karna; Chitra and Vipachitra, Chitraksha, Charuchitra, and Angada, Durmada, and Dushpradharsh
